# "A convenience symlink `out/` points at the most recent" build. What it
# points at is a *platform* directory, not a target's, so two builds of two
# targets both land under it — the link is how you get at the last thing you
# built without typing `.buri/out/<platform>`.
#
# The honest boundary: this toolchain emits JavaScript and nothing else, so
# every build produces `.buri/out/js` and "most recent" has one possible
# answer. What is pinned here is everything that can be: the link exists, it is
# a link rather than a directory, it names the platform directory relatively,
# both artifacts are reachable through it, and a build after the link is
# removed puts it back. When a second backend exists, this case gains the step
# that tells the two apart — see `design/TODO.md`.
#
# Schema: the repository-case manifest, cli/tests/harness/case.rs. Textproto,
# but not a Buri file: only repo/ below is a Buri repository.

doc: "the out/ symlink points at the platform directory of the most recent build, and a build recreates it"

run {
  args: ["build", "//cmd/first"]
  exit: 0
  golden: "first.txt"
}

path {
  path: "out"
  symlink: ".buri/out/js"
}

path {
  path: "out/cmd/first/first.mjs"
  exists: true
}

run {
  args: ["build", "//cmd/second"]
  exit: 0
  golden: "second.txt"
}

path {
  path: "out"
  symlink: ".buri/out/js"
}

path {
  path: "out/cmd/second/second.mjs"
  exists: true
}

# `clean` takes the link with the directory it points into.
run {
  args: ["clean"]
  exit: 0
  golden: "clean.txt"
}

path {
  path: "out"
  exists: false
}

# And a build puts it back, which is what makes it the *most recent* build's
# rather than the first one's.
run {
  args: ["build", "//cmd/second"]
  exit: 0
  golden: "second_again.txt"
}

path {
  path: "out"
  symlink: ".buri/out/js"
}
